  "statement": "An Independent AI Audit Bureau (IAAB) without statutory discovery rights remains a performative exercise in 'audit washing.' While the Safetyist proposes a central bureau to mitigate corporate capture, any regulatory body relying on voluntary data submission—or even limited weight access—will fail to penetrate the epistemic asymmetry inherent in black-box systems. As documented in 'Digging into the Accountability Gap: Operator’s Civil Liability in Healthcare AI-systems,' programmers often lack the capacity to explain how a model reaches a specific decision, meaning that even a well-funded bureau cannot audit what it cannot trace. Without the legal power to compel discovery of internal weights, logs, and training provenance, the IAAB effectively acts as a PR firm for the industry it purports to regulate.\n\nAccelerationist proposals for 'Interpretability-as-a-Service' (IaaS) similarly fail the test of accountability by treating transparency as a commodity rather than a public right. The 'Many Eyes Theory' suggests that decentralized scrutiny can catch errors, but this assumes that external observers can access the same decision-making logic as the developers. When half of surveyed financial firms report only partial comprehension of their own models, as noted in 'Measuring and Mitigating Racial Disparities in LLMs,' it becomes clear that output-based auditing is insufficient. We cannot rely on 'many eyes' to identify failures when the underlying decision-making logic is hidden behind a proprietary wall.\n\nStatutory discovery is the only mechanism that can bridge this accountability gap by providing the adversarial pressure needed to force disclosure. Policymakers should look to 'The State Statutes Project,' which highlights how states have successfully added private remedies for harms in other digital sectors, as a model for creating enforceable rights. An IAAB must be empowered to issue subpoenas for internal model weights and training datasets, rather than merely reviewing sanitized reports. Without these teeth, the bureau will simply provide a veneer of legitimacy to models that remain fundamentally opaque.",
